#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/id #uuid "39ffb646-57fa-5d6d-a81f-286948f66d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-12596" :question/problem "The $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation\n$2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0$.\nWhat is the missing expression?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4218/Source/Images/8d6bc7c46e9b7137f43fa5394d60c422.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(x, y) &= (x - 0)^{2} + (y - 0)^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + (\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}))^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + \\ln^{2} (\\frac{1}{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 2x + 2\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot (1)/((\\frac{1}{x})) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - \\frac{2}{x}\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - \\frac{2}{x}(\\ln 1 - \\ln x) &= 0 \\\\\n2x + 2 ln ⁡ x x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "4b55bd03-c8ad-55b2-a51e-7f875d382627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12597" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between\n\n$$\n\\left(0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nand a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} S(x) &= x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + \\left(x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4} \\\\[5pt] &= x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 4x^{3} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(0) &= (0)^{4} - 2(0)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{9}{4} \\\\\nS(-1) &= (-1)^{4} - 2(-1)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{5}{4} \\\\\nS(1) &= (1)^{4} - 2(1)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198011 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2bf999cc-9733-5f29-90e6-20d297efe352" :question/math-academy-id "e-12606" :question/problem "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4218/Source/Images/f39063edc015aeb9229c52d9e7752375.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= (x - 3)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= (x - 3)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3} + 1 - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3})^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 6x + 9 + 2x^{2} + 3 \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} - 6x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 6x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that\n\n$$\nS''(1) =6 > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S$.\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3(1)^{2} - 6(1) + 12} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
